alue.\n\nBut here's a key insight:\n- If I can form **Pure Straight (16 points)** in Characters: need chows of W1-2-3, W4-5-6, W7-8-9.\n- But:\n  - W4\u00d73 is in my meld \u2192 used.\n  - W6: opponent has Peng W6 \u2192 gone.\n  - W5: only 1 left, never seen \u2014 likely unavailable.\n  - So W4-5-6 impossible.\n- W7-8-9: already melded by opponent \u2192 blocked.\n- So Pure Straight is impossible.\n- Similarly, **Mixed Triple Chow**, **Three-Suited Terminal Chows** \u2014 not feasible due to lack of matching structures.\n- Best remains **Full Flush (24 points)** \u2014 provided I eliminate all non-Character tiles.\n\nSo priority: discard **T6, B2, B4, B7**.\n\nAmong these, which is safest?\n- **T6**: already melded by opponent \u2192 very safe.\n- **B2**: B1 is exhausted (0 left) \u2192 cannot form B1-2-3. B3 still exists (2 left), B4=2 \u2192 B2-3-4 possible? Maybe, but no visible signs.\n- But no one has shown interest in B2/B3/B4. Previous player discarded B4, B8 \u2014 suggests weak bamboo.\n- Still, **T6 is safer** because it\u2019s already publicly used in a meld.\n\nThus, **Play T6** is optimal: removes off-suit tile, minimal risk, advances Full Flush.\n\nAlso, after playing T6, I\u2019ll have:\nHand: W2\u00d73, W3, W7, W8, W9, B2, B4, B7\nStill need to shed B2, B4, B7, and ideally W3/W7/W8/W9 later.\n\nBut step by step.\n\nAnswer choice must be from valid actions: [Play W8; Play W3; Play T6; Play B4; Play W9; Play W2; Play B7; Play W7; Play B2]\n\nBest: **Play T6**\n\nReason: It removes a non-character tile safely (since T6 is already melded by an opponent), helping build toward Full Flush (24 points), while avoiding dangerous waits on W7/W8/W9 which are linked to existing opponent melds.\n\nEvaluation: Focus on maximizing fan value via Full Flush; discard off-suit tiles first. T6 is safe to discard due to opponent's meld.\nReason: Playing T6 eliminates a non-Character tile safely (T6 already melded by opponent), advancing toward Full Flush (24 points) while minimizing risk.\nAnswer: Play T6"}
